/-!
# Analytic sets are universally measurable

This file proves Lusin's universal-measurability theorem: every analytic subset of a Polish space
is measurable after completing any s-finite measure defined on a measurable space containing the
Borel sets. Equivalently, it differs from a measurable set by a null set. It also records the
generated-measurable-space consequence used by measurable selection.

The core proof is the capacity argument for finite measures. Write an analytic set as the range of
a continuous map from Baire space. Successively bound the coordinates in the source while spending
a geometric error budget; the image of the resulting compact product captures the range up to
arbitrary outer-measure error. A countable union of these inner compact approximations then differs
from the analytic set by a null set. For an s-finite measure, apply this construction to each finite
component and take the union of the resulting measurable subsets.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.MeasureTheory.analyticSet_setOf_iInf_lt`: the strict sublevel of a pointwise infimum is
  analytic when the corresponding set before projection is measurable;
* `TauCeti.MeasureTheory.AnalyticSet.nullMeasurableSet`: an analytic subset of a Polish space is
  null-measurable for every s-finite measure on a measurable space containing the Borel sets;
* `TauCeti.MeasureTheory.NullMeasurableSet.of_generateFrom_analyticSet`: the same holds for the
  measurable space generated by analytic sets;
* `TauCeti.MeasureTheory.nullMeasurable_of_measurable_generateFrom_analyticSet`: a function
  measurable for that generated measurable space is null-measurable for every such s-finite
  measure.

## Provenance

The proof is adapted from Daniel Lyng's Apache-2.0 Econlib development,
`Econlib/Math/MeasureTheory/AnalyticNullMeasurable.lean`, commit
`003655ccf010cdf44c4f67d6675167b54ce0e9df`. The implementation is reorganized so that proof-only
capacity lemmas are private, while the three reusable consequences remain public.

## References

* A. S. Kechris, *Classical Descriptive Set Theory*, Springer-Verlag, 1995, Theorem 29.7.
-/

